→ Unsupported — not found as a diagnostic criterion or associated feature in DSM-5-TR or ICD-11

Claim 3: If they have a minor commitment later today, they can't relax and are stuck in "waiting mode" all day
→ Unsupported — not established in DSM-5-TR, ICD-11, or NICE as an ADHD symptom

Claim 4: They have a strong sense of justice and fairness, reacting strongly when people do mean things
→ Supported — the ICD-11-based differential diagnosis reference explicitly links ADHD's emotion regulation problems to "a strong sense of justice and low frustration tolerance"

Claim 5: Their hyperactivity is internalized ("in their heads"), they were never physically hyperactive, and were misdiagnosed with an anxiety disorder
→ Partially supported — ICD-11 does note hyperactivity in adolescents/adults often presents as internal "feelings of physical restlessness" rather than overt motor hyperactivity, giving the internalization idea some basis; however, ICD-11's actual boundary discussion of anxiety and fear-related disorders doesn't support the framing that internalized hyperactivity routinely causes anxiety-disorder misdiagnosis

